    "product_id": "4866ebf0-081e-4ce8-bfd9-b60f449d8c56",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 201834\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1135 AM PDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Multiple rounds of rain and storms expected over the Sierra, Lower \n  Great Basin and NW Arizona this afternoon through Thursday. Flash \n  Flooding possible under repeated storm activity. \n  \n* Increasing confidence for a dry conditions and seasonable \n  temperatures this weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Next Monday.\n\nAn active weather pattern is setting up for the Desert Southwest \nthis week as a strong trough slowly approaches the west coast \nimpacting local weather through the end of the week. For this \nafternoon and tonight, mainly dry conditions expected for most \ndesert valleys while the first round of the thunderstorms is \nexpected over Inyo, southern Nye, and Esmeralda counties...more \nisolated convection possible in higher terrain further east. The \nentire area remains ahead of the approaching trough with strong \nupper level diffluence and enhanced lift from the left exit region \nof a 100 kt jet streak. Moisture advection northward is great for \nthis time of year with PW of 0.7-1.00 inches engulfing the area. \nMUCAPE of 300-700 j/kg combined with 0-6km shear exceeding 50 kt \nwill be more than sufficient for more organized clusters of showers \nand storms. DCAPE values 800-1000+ j/kg may also lead to a localized \nstrong wind threat in a few storms this afternoon and evening on top \nof the heavy rain threat. Best forcing for ascent will be in the \nhigher terrain of Inyo county and Death Valley, Southern and Western \nNye County and Esmeralda County. At the moment confidence is not \nhigh enough in a widespread Flash Flood threat to warrant the \nissuance of any type of watch, however a low threat does exist in \nareas that see repeated rounds of storms. Lingering lighter shower \nactivity may persist into the night due to such strong lift and \nupper level dynamics\n\nFor Wednesday the upper level low will be approaching the Central \nCalifornia Coast. Precipitation chances are higher across the board \ndue to additional moisture and colder air aloft, but the same places \nwill be favored in addition to Mohave County. A strong southerly \n50kt low to mid level jet sets up bringing copious amounts of \nmoisture northward into NW Arizona. As waves of energy rotate around \nthe trough and into the region, expect showers and storms to begin \ndeveloping early Wednesday morning across Mohave County and \npotentially Inyo and Esmeralda counties, with the most robust \nconvection likely during the afternoon hours. Shear, instability, \nand moisture will be similar if not better than parameters on \nTuesday, resulting in the same threat...flash flooding, gusty winds, \nheavy rain, and lightning. Highest confidence in any Flash Flooding \nis currently over Northern Mohave County where better lift and \nmoisture availability should result in more robust storms; Therefore \na Flash Flood Watch has been issued for that area Wednesday and \nThursday. While showers and storms are expected Wednesday afternoon \nover Inyo, Esmeralda, and Nye counties, more isolated convective \ncoverage should keep the flash flood threat lower. Outside of any \nconvection, most of the Mohave Desert and its valleys should be \nfairly warm and dry with temperatures a few degrees above normal for \nthis time of year.\n\nFinally on Thursday, the upper low shifts inland into Central \nCalifornia and Nevada with the main vort max bringing widespread \nthreat of showers and storms to much of the Lower great basin. Steep \nlapse rates and sufficient instability from cold air advection aloft \nwill allow for convective showers and a few storms through the day \non Thursday. This will likely be the best chance for any rainfall in \nthe Mojave Desert valleys; however guidance has been consistent in \nshowing a dry slot associated with the incoming trough keeping the \nmajority of the precipitation away from the Mohave Desert and \nColorado River Valley with a 40-70% chance of 0.10\" of rain or less. \nOverall, best odds of precipitation impacts are in the southern \nGreat Basin, Inyo County, and northern Mohave County where higher \nchances of total rainfall 1.00\" exist. Outside of any storms, gusty \nsouth-southwest winds (20-40 mph) will accompany this system as well \nThursday and Friday, but any impacts should be confined to the local \nlakes. Temperatures drop about 10 degrees from earlier in the week \nwith widespread highs Thursday into the weekend in the 70s for \ndesert valleys. \n\nThere is a minor wintry component of this system as snow levels \nbottom out around 8kft in the Sierra. However, precipitation will \nlargely be done by the time colder air filters in , so any impacts \nare expected to be confined to elevations above 9kft where forecast \nsnow totals range from 3-10\" (the higher amounts near the crest). \n\nBy Saturday, ensemble guidance has the area firmly on the backside \nof the trough with a dry northerly flow that quickly scours out \nmoisture and precipitation chances, and produces some gusty north \nwinds. On Sunday and Monday, the flow weakens and becomes more zonal \nor southwesterly, keeping us dry with light winds and slightly below-\nnormal temperatures.\n\n\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill be generally light and diurnal through the day today, with \nlight easterly winds this morning, light-to-breezy southeasterly \nwinds this afternoon around 8 kts, and light southwesterly winds \novernight. Gusty south-southeasterly winds pick up Wednesday \nafternoon with gust speeds topping out between 25 and 30 kts. FEW-\nSCT aoa 10 kft through the TAF period.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light and diurnal winds \nin the Las Vegas Valley and Colorado River Valley through the day \ntoday, with gusty south-southeasterly winds Wednesday afternoon to \n30 kts. KBIH will gust from the south-southeast this afternoon to 30 \nkts with the potential for gusts from variable directions due to \nvicinity thunderstorms. 30 percent chance of showers and \nthunderstorms over the KBIH terminal, with best chances between 01 \nand 05Z. Gusty east winds at KDAG to 25 kts, with potential \ndirection changes due to vicinity showers between 21 and 02Z. FEW-\nSCT aoa 10 kft, though expect BKN aoa 8 kft with thunderstorm \nactivity overhead.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
